      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
AIR SEALING

Overall, your house is built quite tight from an air leakage perspective, and I have identified a few locations that can be addressed:

** The largest source is the open combustion air pipe (5" in diameter) located beside the furnace in your mechanical room on the ground floor - this pipe is likely a significant cause of this room being cold. With a furnace upgrade to a new condensing furnace (propane or oil) it would eliminate the combustion air pipe.

** The co-vented chimney for the furnace and DHW will also be eliminated with a furnace and hot water tank upgrade, which will further reduce the air leakage.

** Around the attic hatch is a small source of air leakage upstairs. This can be sealed with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack".

Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a modest 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss, but it could result in a larger reduction. This is estimated to save you $90 per year and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.8 GJ. Although the savings aren't huge, the increase in comfort downstairs will be noticeable.

There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $715 and $1300 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.

An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.
</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>
MAIN WALLS

The main exterior walls of your home, appear to be 2x6 construction with batt insulation (R20), which was confirmed by the homeowner. In the basement, the walls are a concrete foundation with a pony wall on top, and an additional 2x6 wall constructed on the interior from floor to ceiling. For your interest, I have modelled adding 3 additional inches (R15) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation to every exterior surface, which would include the inside portion of the garage walls. Due to the large amount of labour involved in re-siding a home, and the high cost, this recommendation should only be taken in conjunction with a siding upgrade.

Savings: By adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls, you could potentially save $ 470 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 14 GJ, and qualify for up to a $2,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program insulation rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.
</mainWalls>
                <foundation>
Basement

Your basement is buillt with a 2x6 pony wall, assumed to be filled with batt insulation (R20) on top of a concrete foundation, and has an interior 2x6 wall as well, that is also estimated to be R20. I've recommended a 3" XPS upgrade to all exterior walls, which would include the above grade portion of the basement walls. 

I did model an upgrade to your foundation floor, but the savings were minimal. However by insulating and air sealing the floor joists you would increase comfort downstairs.

Savings: The savings for this pony wall upgrade are minimal, at $90 per year, but realistically if you're adding insulation to the upper walls you would also add insulation to the pony wall section.

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program insulation rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 

The rebate amount that you would qualify for would be 50% of the foundation walls at R15 which equals a rebate of $625. There are also rebates from the Canada Greener Homes program, if you were to sign up for that.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
</foundation>
                <VentilationSystem>
HRV INSTALLATION

During the blower door test it was discovered that your house is quite air tight, at 1.99 air changes per hour.

If your house was being built today (including the renovation upgrades outlined in this report), it would have a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed, rather than relying on natural air infiltration, or exhaust devices to provide a means of fresh air for your home.

Savings: Installing an Energy Star certified HRV that is a minimum 70% efficient and includes ECM blower fan motors is estimated to increase your energy costs by $200 per year and increase your annual energy usage by 5.1 GJ. By installing a HRV the ventilation of your house will be better managed and the comfort and indoor air quality for the occupants will be much better.
</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>
Propane Instantaneous Upgrade

You currently have a 121 litre conventional, oil fired hot water tank. Using oil heating appliances is costly and you may want to consider replacing your existing hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing propane-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.

Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, you could potentially save approximately $160 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.
</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
FURNACE UPGRADE

Your current oil furnace is a mid efficient, furnace, with an open combustion air pipe which means it's dumping cold air into the furnace room constantly. Your furnace is the only source of heat in the home, therefore I have modelled a couple options for you to consider.

PROPANE FURNACE

I've modelled upgrading to an ENERGY STAR® qualified, high-efficiency, condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 97% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.

This upgrade is currently eligible for a $1,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). Full program details are outlined on our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program.

Savings:

By replacing your current oil furnace with a 97% AFUE condensing propane furnace that incorporates an ECM fan blower - and considering current oil and propane prices - you could potentially save $1800 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 46 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

By upgrading to the above furnace you would also qualify for a $1600 rebate from the Canada Greener Homes Grant.

SUPPLEMENTARY HEATING SYSTEM

PELLET STOVE

Since you have no secondary heat source in your home I modelled the savings if you were to switch to heating with biomass, using a pellet stove. I modelled it as if it were providing 50% of the heat for your home, and located on the colder, basement floor. I also modelled it as a sealed combustion unit, meaning it has no interaction with interior air.

If you were to complete this upgrade, and keep your existing older furnace, you would see savings of $1700 per year!

There is a $1000 rebate available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program from AEA. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>
SOLAR PV

SOLAR PV GENERATION:  Hydro Community

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.

Savings: By installing a 5 kW solar photovoltaic system on the south facing roof of your house you could potentially save $860 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 10 GJ. 

Please speak with a solar installer to discuss appropriate sizing of the system for your home.

There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program for electricity generation systems installed in a Hydro Community, including Yellowknife, however there are incentives available from the Canada Greener Homes Program

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a photovoltaic solar panel. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards.
</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>
Your house has two older 13 liter toilets that could be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush!

Savings: By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings. 

There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>
ADDITIONAL COMMENTS

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.

Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.

ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.

Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:

 In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report
 If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.
 Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit
 All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional
 It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:
     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*
     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards
     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home
*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.
 All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.

ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test = Pass

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 2.6 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not likely to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home. However your furnace and your DHW tank vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting. An upgrade to the suggested furnace and DHW get their combustion air directly from outside, thus there is no worry about bac kdrafting. 

During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:
       
        - two bathroom fans
        - a clothes dryer
      
You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs  Yellowknife

The fuel values below may be different than current prices, as they have been averaged over the past 5 years to account for fuel price fluctuations over time. These prices have been used to calculate energy costs and energy savings in Yellowknife, for your report:

Electricity - $0.32 per kWh
Oil - $1.24 per liter 
Propane - $0.75 per liter delivered
Wood - $410 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested
Pellets - $5.79 per bag, $351.29 per tonne bulk delivered


